  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nMoisture from what remains of Isaias continues to battle high \npressure building across the north. Dry air has been winning \nthat battle so far today, but will gradually give way to rain \nthru this evening. The best chance of rain remains south of the \nmtns, and most of the rainfall confined to southern NH where \namounts near 0.5 inches are possible. Otherwise rainfall totals \nwill drop off quickly to the north, generally less than 0.1 \ninches north of the Lakes Region in NH and Portland in western \nMaine.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nOn Mon a bit of a stationary boundary will remain draped across\nthe forecast area and may provide a focus for isolated showers \nthru the day. Overall it should be mostly dry across the area.\n\nMon night a backdoor cold front will drive southwest across the\nregion as a strong high pressure branches into Quebec. This \noccurs just before the next round of precip moves into the area.\nAn active push of cold air combined with precip should ensure \ncold air damming remains in place thru Tue. I have taken temps \ndown during the day. The NBM often struggles with CAD \nsituations, and this appears to be no different. A blend of the \n10th percentile NBM temps and raw GFS produces a pretty fair \nrepresentation to start with. Guidance at this time focuses on \nthe northern half to two thirds of the area for a solid \nbeneficial rainfall of at least 0.5 inches. This is somewhat \nlower confidence however as occasional model runs have been \nfarther south. So I have greater confidence in the PoP than the \nQPF at this point. Also of note will be very chilly temps in \nparts of the western Maine mtns. Forecast soundings remain \nsupportive of some wintry precip types. As the system gets into \nthe range of more hi-res guidance the WAA aloft is getting \nstronger. This may take snow out of the mix more but may leave \nsleet and freezing rain, especially mid-slope in the mtns, on \nthe table. Precip will begin to wind down Tue night.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\n\nLow pressure begins to lift northeast toward the Canadian \nMaritimes on Wednesday, sending a cold front through in the \nmorning, which will bring an end to what is left of the \nwidespread rainfall. However, the northwest winds behind the \nfront will result in periods of upslope precipitation in the \nmountains, which should initially be rain, but these could \nswitch to snow showers Wednesday night into early Thursday as \ncolder air move in aloft.\n\nFor the rest of the area on Wednesday, forecast soundings \nsuggest a mix of partly sunny and mostly cloudy skies with a few\nadditional showers as a shortwave pushes through. Otherwise, it\nwill be somewhat breezy with northwest wind gusts to around \n20-30 mph, and high temperatures will be mainly in the 60s with \nsome guidance even advertising low 70s for parts of southern NH.\n\nA secondary front crosses Wednesday night or early Thursday, \nand the tightening gradient between the low over the maritimes \nand high pressure building in from the west will result in even \nbreezier conditions. Gusts to around 25-30 mph are possible \nWednesday night, and then 25 to 35 mph during the day Thursday, \nperhaps closer to 40 mph at times. Drier air will also move in, \nso we'll likely see more sunshine with upslope precip in the \nmountains coming to an end. Cold air advection will limit highs \nmostly to the 50s.\n\nA cold night is possible Thursday night as high pressure builds\nbriefly overhead, but it quickly exits to the east ahead of the\nnext front and low pressure. There's strong support in the \nensembles for most of the area to see measurable precipitation \nFriday into Saturday, so the high NBM PoPs of 60-80% are \njustified. This will be rain for most, but some solutions are \nfarther south with the low track, which may be cold enough for \nlight snow accumulations across the north. Guidance favors drier\nweather returning by Sunday with cooler temperatures moving in \nfor the weekend.",
